FORK(2)                        P32W Unix                         FORK(2)

NAME
       fork - create a new process

SYNOPSIS
       #include <unistd.h>

       pid_t fork(void);

DESCRIPTION
       fork makes a new process, the child, which is a copy of the
       calling process, the parent. The child runs the same program
       from the same point; the two differ in these ways:

       -  fork returns the child's process ID in the parent and 0
          in the child.
       -  The child has a new process ID; its parent process ID is
          the caller's.
       -  The child's CPU times (times(2)) start at zero, it has no
          alarm pending (alarm(2)), no signal pending, and it is not
          being traced (ptrace(2)).

       Everything else is inherited: the address space (shared
       copy-on-write, so a page is copied only when one of the two
       writes it; text is shared outright), the open file
       descriptors (which share their file offsets with the
       parent's), the current directory, the real and effective
       user and group IDs, the file creation mask, the controlling
       terminal, and the signal actions, caught, ignored or
       default.

RETURN VALUE
       0 in the child; the child's process ID in the parent. On
       failure -1 in the parent, no child is made, and errno says
       why.

ERRORS
       EAGAIN  The process table is full (64 processes).
       ENOMEM  There is no memory for the child's kernel stack or
               page table.

EXAMPLES
       A shell runs a command roughly so:

              if ((pid = fork()) == 0) {
                  execvp(argv[0], argv);
                  _exit(127);
              }
              while (wait(&status) != pid)
                  ;

SEE ALSO
       exec(2), exit(2), wait(2), signal(2), sh(1)

BUGS
       There is no vfork. There is no per-user process limit: one
       user can fill the process table.
